            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Search inside your home. Keep an eye out for a metal box, usually flush with the wall. The box will have a metal door to keep the breakers or fuses protected. Check your garage for the circuit breaker or fuse box. It might also be in a storage room, utility room, the basement, or a hallway.<ref>[v161397_b01]. 29 April 2020.</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Search outside if you can’t find it. Depending on how old your house is, your circuit breaker or fuse box could be outside.<ref>[v161397_b01]. 29 April 2020.</ref> Start by checking next to the meter box for your house.[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Typically if a fuse blows, it's because too many devices or appliances are pulling power from that circuit. If you're having a recurring problem with this, you may need to install a new circuit breaker to split the load.<ref>[v161397_b01]. 29 April 2020.</ref>",
                    "#* If you trip the breaker from plugging in too many appliances, try using a different outlet. If that does not solve the problem, call an electrician, as you may need to have a new circuit breaker installed.<ref>[v161397_b01]. 29 April 2020.</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Search inside your home. Keep an eye out for a metal box, usually flush with the wall. The box will have a metal door to keep the breakers or fuses protected. Check your garage for the circuit breaker or fuse box. It might also be in a storage room, the basement, or a hallway.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Search outside if you can’t find it. Depending on how old your house is, your circuit breaker or fuse box could be outside. Check next to the meter box for your house.[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If you trip the breaker from plugging in too many appliances, try using a different outlet. If that does not solve the problem, call an electrician.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
