        {
            "id": "24400595",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "22:08, 17 April 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,676 bytes)",
            "change": 533,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Search inside your home. Keep an eye out for a metal box, usually flush with the wall. The box will have a metal door to keep the breakers or fuses protected. Check your garage for the circuit breaker or fuse box. It might also be in a storage room, the basement, or a hallway.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you can't find your box in one of these areas, look again or see if you can spot where the power connects to your house from outside. Check for your box in the surrounding areas.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#*If you live in an apartment, check in a cupboard or hallway.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#Search outside if you can’t find it. Depending on how old your house is, your circuit breaker or fuse box could be outside. Check next to the meter box for your house.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If you have trouble finding your circuit breaker or fuse box, ask a neighbor where theirs is. If you live in a neighborhood with houses built in the same era, the boxes may be in similar locations.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#*Call an electrician if you can't find your circuit breaker or fuse box. Some boxes can be hard to find depending on additions or remodels made to the house.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#*Older homes tend to have fuse boxes. If you have a particularly large house, you may even have multiple fuse boxes.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "#*If you don't unplug any appliances before replacing the fuse, you run the risk of blowing the new fuse as well.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "#Turn off the main power in the fuse box. You should see a main power switch in the fuse box that can be toggled between On/Off. Wear gloves and rubber soled shoes when working in the fuse box. Be sure to remove any jewelry before replacing fuses.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref> It is possible to replace fuses without turning the main power off, but it is dangerous. Exercise caution when working in the fuse box.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If there isn’t a switch to turn off the power, there will be a fuse block, usually in the center of the top row. Pull it out and check to see if it is labeled “on” and one side and “off” on the other. If so, reinsert it with the “off” side facing up. If not, leave it out while you replace the fuse, then reinsert it.",
                    "#*The blown fuse will be cloudy or the metal line in the fuse will be broken.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*If you don't have a circuit list, you may have to guess which fuse is blown. If you pull a fuse out and no other circuits lose power, you've found the blown fuse.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "#Replace the fuse. To replace the fuse, simply screw the new fuse into the socket. Make sure you replace the fuse with a new one of the same amperage. Do not replace a blown fuse with one of a higher rating.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Prepare-a-Gas-Powered-Lawnmower-Step-17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn on the main power. Once you have replaced the fuse, switch the main power back on. If you have a fuse block instead of a switch, replace the block with the side labeled “on” facing up. If the fuse blows again, call an electrician to inspect your wiring.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*If the fuse does not blow, plug in any appliances you were using. If the fuse blows, there could be a problem with the appliance. There could also be too many appliances plugged into one circuit.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "#*Some circuit breakers have a red or orange mark on them that is easy to see when the switch is off. This can help you locate the tripped breaker."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "#Search inside. Keep an eye out for a metal box, usually flush with the wall. The box will have a metal door to keep the breakers or fuses protected. Check your garage for the circuit breaker or fuse box. It might also be in a storage room, the basement, or a hallway.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If you can't find your box in one of these areas, look again or see if you can spot where the power connects to your house from outside. Check for your box in the surrounding areas.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#* If you live in an apartment, check in a cupboard or hallway.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#Search outside. Depending on how old your house is, your circuit breaker or fuse box could be outside. Check next to the meter box for your house.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If you have trouble finding your circuit breaker or fuse box, ask a neighbor where theirs is. If you live in a neighborhood with houses built in the same era, the boxes may be in similar locations.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#* Call an electrician if you can't find your circuit breaker or fuse box. Some boxes can be hard to find depending on additions or remodels made to the house.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "#* Older homes tend to have fuse boxes. If you have a particularly large house, you may even have multiple fuse boxes.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "#* If you don't unplug any appliances before replacing the fuse, you run the risk of blowing the new fuse as well.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "#Turn off the main power in the fuse box. You should see a main power switch in the fuse box that can be toggled between On/Off. Wear gloves and rubber soled shoes when working in the fuse box.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Be sure to remove any jewelry before replacing fuses.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "#* It is possible to replace fuses without turning the main power off, but it is dangerous. Exercise caution when working in the fuse box.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "#* The blown fuse will be cloudy or the metal line in the fuse will be broken.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "#* If you don't have a circuit list, you may have to guess which fuse is blown. If you pull a fuse out and no other circuits lose power, you've found the blown fuse.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "# Replace the fuse. To replace the fuse, simply screw the new fuse into the socket. Make sure you replace the fuse with a new one of the same amperage. Do not replace a blown fuse with one of a higher rating.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Prepare-a-Gas-Powered-Lawnmower-Step-17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn on the main power. Once you have replaced the fuse, switch the main power back on. If the fuse blows again, call an electrician to inspect your wiring.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* If the fuse does not blow, plug in any appliances you were using. If the fuse blows, there could be a problem with the appliance. There could also be too many appliances plugged into one circuit.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    ""
                ]
            }
        },
