        {
            "id": "19191180",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "19:21, 4 January 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,110 bytes)",
            "change": 586,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Search inside. Keep an eye out for a metal box, usually flush with the wall. The box will have a metal door to keep the breakers or fuses protected. Check your garage for the circuit breaker or fuse box. It might also be in a storage room, the basement, or a hallway.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Search outside. Depending on how old your house is, your circuit breaker or fuse box could be outside. Check next to the meter box for your house.<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Determine if you have a fuse box or a circuit breaker box. Once you've found your box, open the door. If you see rows of switches, you have a circuit breaker box. Fuses are round and screw into sockets in the fuse box, much like a light bulb.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Unplug any appliances in the same area where power loss occurred. If you lost power in your bedroom, unplug everything in that room before replacing any fuses.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn off the main power in the fuse box. You should see a main power switch in the fuse box that can be toggled between On/Off. Wear gloves and rubber soled shoes when working in the fuse box.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find the broken fuse. In each fuse box there should be a circuit list that tells you what fuses control what circuits. This will give you an idea of what fuse may have blown. Find the fuse that corresponds with the area of the house that lost power.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn on the main power. Once you have replaced the fuse, switch the main power back on. If the fuse blows again, call an electrician to inspect your wiring.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Unplug any appliances in the area without power. When you trip a breaker and lose power to a room, unplug everything in that room.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Find the \"tripped\" breaker. You can spot the breaker in question by finding the switch that is out of line with the other switches. If you have power, your breakers should all be in the \"on\" position. The tripped breaker will either be flipped to \"off\" or it will not be fully on.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Reset the breaker. Flip the tripped breaker all the way off before turning it back on. Many breakers will not reset unless they have been turned off completely.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Test your power. Once you have flipped the breaker back on, plug your appliances back in. If you trip the breaker again, there may be an issue with your appliance, or you could be plugging too many appliances into one circuit.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 12.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "# Search inside. Keep an eye out for a metal box, usually flush with the wall. The box will have a metal door to keep the breakers or fuses protected. Check your garage for the circuit breaker or fuse box. It might also be in a storage room, the basement, or a hallway.[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 1.jpg|center]]<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "# Search outside. Depending on how old your house is, your circuit breaker or fuse box could be outside. Check next to the meter box for your house.[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 4.jpg|center]]<ref>http://www.mynrma.com.au/emergency-home-assist/electricity-emergencies/locate-the-circuit-breaker-in-your-home.htm</ref>",
                    "# Determine if you have a fuse box or a circuit breaker box. Once you've found your box, open the door. If you see rows of switches, you have a circuit breaker box. Fuses are round and screw into sockets in the fuse box, much like a light bulb.[[Image:Find the Fuse Box or Circuit Breaker Box Step 5.jpg|center]]<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "# Unplug any appliances in the same area where power loss occurred. If you lost power in your bedroom, unplug everything in that room before replacing any fuses.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "# Turn off the main power in the fuse box. You should see a main power switch in the fuse box that can be toggled between On/Off. Wear gloves and rubber soled shoes when working in the fuse box.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "# Find the broken fuse. In each fuse box there should be a circuit list that tells you what fuses control what circuits. This will give you an idea of what fuse may have blown. Find the fuse that corresponds with the area of the house that lost power.<ref>http://www.visionaware.org/info/everyday-living/home-repairs/gils-guide-to-home-repairs/fuses-and-circuit-breakers/1235</ref>",
                    "# Turn on the main power. Once you have replaced the fuse, switch the main power back on. If the fuse blows again, call an electrician to inspect your wiring.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "# Unplug any appliances in the area without power. When you trip a breaker and lose power to a room, unplug everything in that room.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "# Find the \"tripped\" breaker. You can spot the breaker in question by finding the switch that is out of line with the other switches. If you have power, your breakers should all be in the \"on\" position. The tripped breaker will either be flipped to \"off\" or it will not be fully on.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "# Reset the breaker. Flip the tripped breaker all the way off before turning it back on. Many breakers will not reset unless they have been turned off completely.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>",
                    "# Test your power. Once you have flipped the breaker back on, plug your appliances back in. If you trip the breaker again, there may be an issue with your appliance, or you could be plugging too many appliances into one circuit.<ref>http://www.livingwithmyhome.com/101-home-tips/fuse.aspx</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
